# Ambient-HttpClient allowlist (issue #1311).
#
# `@inject HttpClient` in a Blazor component binds the AMBIENT client — the
# one the auth service itself uses, so wiring the bearer-token handler into
# it would be circular DI. It carries NO Authorization header. A component
# that injects it and calls an authenticated endpoint gets a silent 401,
# not a build error and not a thrown exception — the failure mode is often a
# convincing empty state ("No trusted lists imported yet.") rather than a
# visible error, which is why it has bitten three times without being
# caught by review:
#   #1165/#1166 — the whole F126/F128 device-pairing surface, dead for every citizen.
#   #1167       — F181 admin clients; ListAsync swallowed the 401 into an empty list.
#   #1310       — the PWA's OpenID4VP direct_post.
#
# This gate is FORWARD-ONLY — the three incidents above are its motivation,
# not proof it would have caught them. It scans `.razor` for ambient
# `HttpClient` injection only, so it would not have caught #1167 (a typed
# client missing its auth handler in a `.cs` registration) and would simply
# have seeded #1310's Present.razor onto this allowlist rather than flagged
# it, since the ambient injection predated the fix. What it does is stop the
# *next* `.razor` ambient-injection site from landing unnoticed.
#
# Enforced by scripts/check-ambient-httpclient.ps1 (CI: ambient-httpclient-gate).
#
# THIS LIST IS A RATCHET — it may only shrink, never grow. Every entry below
# is a site that has NOT yet been audited, or is deliberately anonymous (see
# the two annotated exceptions). Adding an entry here to make a build pass is
# not acceptable — audit the call site (does it hit an authenticated
# endpoint? does a 401 there fail loudly or silently?) and either move it to
# a typed client wired with BearerTokenHandler, or annotate it here as
# deliberately anonymous with the reasoning, in the same PR.
#
# One relative path per line (forward slashes); trailing # comments allowed.

src/Apps/Sorcha.UI/Sorcha.UI.Components.User/Components/CredentialGate/CredentialGateComponent.razor   # DELIBERATE — GET /api/presentations/{id}/disclosed-claims?token=...; that route is .AllowAnonymous() and the token itself is the credential (single-use, TTL-bound, bound to one presentationRequestId). Attaching the citizen's bearer here would add nothing — the endpoint deliberately authorises on the token, not the caller's session.
src/Apps/Sorcha.UI/Sorcha.UI.Components.User/Components/EnrolGate/EnrolGateComponent.razor              # DELIBERATE — GET /api/auth/whoami; a 401 IS the cold-start answer (not signed in). Attaching auth would turn an expected 401 into a redirect-to-login and break the F126 cold-start gate.
